  "body": "The description for the ApplicationCache [error event](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#event-appcache-error) states:\n\n> The manifest was a 404 or 410 page, so the attempt to cache the application has been aborted.\n\nThe event is also described as \"Last event in sequence\".\nIf we then look at the description of the [obsolete event](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#event-appcache-obsolete), it states:\n\n> The manifest was found to have become a 404 or 410 page, so the application cache is being deleted.\n\nWhat is the difference here?\nBut most confusingly: the obsolete event is also marked as \"Last event in sequence\".\n\nThe events table in the specs suggests that the error event is last in the sequence of events. Does that mean it is never fired, if the manifest is a 404 page? (As it would be handled by the obsolete event, which already is \"Last event in sequence\".)\n\nThe description for the error event also states:\n\n> A fatal error occurred while fetching the resources listed in the manifest.\n\nWhat does \"fatal error\" mean in this context? Is a 404 of one of the resources listed in the manifest considered a fatal error? Does that mean (as suggested by [some](http://www.html5rocks.com/en/tutorials/appcache/beginner/)) that the whole cache update process fails, if one resource listed in the manifest is missing?\n",
